      "textEn": "506\n\nYah - who; Avadhit - killed; Swa - own; Swasu - of sister; Tokan - to the children; Kroshtyaah - shouting; Asu-trup -\nSelfish; Khalah - cruel; Kim Nu - what; Swit - without doubt; Tat - to him; Prajinam - of the people; Vah - from you; Kushalam -\nEfficiency; Vimrishamah - we should guess. That cruel, selfish Kansa killed his crying, grieving sister's children\nin her presence. So then, we, who are their subjects, why should we ask about their well-being?\nCan we? Thus, with the pure speech, he was well-adorned in the mind. Akrurah pariprishten jahavadhvaparishramam. 43. Meaning of the word:\n\nIttham—in this way; Su-nritaya—extremely truthful and good; Vacha—with words; Nanden—by Nand Maharaj; Su—in a good way;\nSabhajitah—honored; Akrurah—by Akrur; Parishishten—when asked; Jahau—removed; Adhva—of the path; Parishramam—\nTiredness. Having been honored by Nand Maharaj with these truthful and sweet words of inquiry, Akrur forgot his\njourney's fatigue.\nThus, under the tenth canto of Srimad Bhagavat, \"The Arrival of Akrura in Vrindavan,\"\ncomposed by the humble servants of Shri Shrimad A.C. Bhaktivedanta Swami Prabhupada, the thirty-eighth chapter\nwas fulfilled. Chapter thirty-nine.\n\nDarshan by Akrur.\n\nIn this chapter, it is told how Akrur informed Lord Krishna and Lord Balram about Kansa's plans and his actions in Mathura. When Krishna left Mathura, the Gopis\nhow, distressed, began to weep, and how Akrur had a vision of Vishnu's abode in the water of the Yamuna. When Krishna and Balram greatly honored Akrur and seated him comfortably on the throne,\nAkrur felt that all the desires that had arisen in his mind while traveling to Vrindavan were now\nall fulfilled. After having dinner in the evening, Krishna asked Akrur if his journey was pleasant"
