      "textEn": "520\n\nWith whom they used to return to Braj, their hair and their garland would become blurred by the dust rising from the hooves of the cows. When he played the flute, he would captivate our hearts with his laughing, rounded countenance.\nHe would enchant us. Srisuka said:\nAnd having spoken, the separated ones, greatly\nThe Braj women, with their minds attached to Krishna. Having abandoned shame, he cried out in a sweet voice.\nGovinda, Damodara, and Madhava. 31. Meaning of the word\n\nShri-Shuka said—Shukadeva Goswami said; Evam—In this way; Bruvanaah—Speaking; Viraha—With feelings of separation;\nAturaah—Anguished; Bhrusham—Extremely; Vraja-strinah—Women of Vraja; Krishna—From Krishna; Vishakta—Attached; Manasaah—Mind\nWali; Visrijya—Renouncing; Lajjam—Shame; Ruruduhsma—Started shouting; Su-swaram—Loudly; Govinda Damodara Madhava\nIti—Hey Govinda, Hey Damodara, Hey Madhava. Shukdev Goswami said: After saying these words, the women of Braj, who were so devoted to Krishna, became extremely agitated by their impending separation.\nShe forgot all shame and modesty,\nAnd shouted loudly, \"Hey Govind, Hey Damodar, Hey Madhav.\" Meaning: For a long time, the Gopis had hidden their love for Krishna. But now,\nwhen Krishna was departing, the Gopis became so anxious that they could not hide their feelings. The women weep, and the sun shines brightly. Akrurashchodayamas kritaitraadiko ratham. 32. Meaning of the word\n\nStri-nam—of women; evam—in this way; rudantinaam—crying; udite—rising; savitri—Sun; ath—\nThen; Akrurah—Akrur; chodayam aas—started; krita—completed; maitra-adikah—morning worship and ethical work;\nRatham—on the chariot..\n\nBut even after the Gopis continued to weep in this manner, Akrur, after completing the morning worship and other\ntasks, started driving his chariot. Meaning: According to some Vaishnava Acharyas, while taking Krishna to Mathura, Akrur committed an offense by not giving consolation to the Gopis.\nDue to this crime, Akrur had to forcibly leave Dwarka later.\nAnd at the time of the Syamantaka Mani incident, he had to separate from Krishna. At that time, Akrur was in Varanasi"
